1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
The first animal humans domesticated was built to kill us. It had powerful jaws, excellent night vision, a nose capable

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
of detecting what we could not see, and enough patience to follow prey for hours. It was a wolf.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Somewhere near the end of the ice age, one population of wolves crossed an invisible boundary. Its descendants stopped being merely wild animals living

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:31,000
near humans and became something entirely new, dogs. But no prehistoric hunter woke up one morning, pointed at a

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
wolf, and announced the invention of the pet. There were no cages, breeding manuals, or bags of chicken flavored

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
rewards. Humans were still hunter gatherers. Many moved regularly, stored little food, and competed with wolves

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:54,000
for the same prey. Yet thousands of years before anyone domesticated sheep, cattle or goats, people somehow formed a

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:02,000
lasting alliance with one of their most dangerous rivals. How? The answer begins with an important distinction. Taming is

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
not domestication. You can capture a wild animal, raise it from infancy, and teach it to tolerate humans. That

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:18,000
individual may become calm, useful, or even affectionate. Its offspring, however, are still genetically wild.

11
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Domestication happens across generations. Humans alter which animals survive and reproduce.

12
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Sometimes this selection is deliberate.

13
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Often it is not. Either way, animals living around people begin evolving differently from their wild relatives.

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Their behavior changes first. Later, their bodies may follow. A tame wolf is still a wolf. A dog belongs to a

15
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:54,000
population whose ancestors spent generations adapting to human life. That transformation was probably so gradual that no one living through it could have identified the exact moment it happened.

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:02,000
The first dogs may not have looked dramatically different from wolves. The earliest domestication was written in behavior long before it became obvious

17
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:10,000
in bones. This is why the date remains uncertain. Archaeologists have found ancient cannid skulls more than 30,000

18
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:19,000
years old with features that some researchers interpret as doglike. One famous example came from Goyette cave in Belgium. Another came from the Alai

19
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Mountains in Siberia. Their snouts appeared shorter or broader than those of typical wolves. Their teeth and skull proportions looked unusual. But unusual does not necessarily mean domesticated.

20
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Ice Age wolves were diverse.

21
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Some populations had body shapes that no longer exist. A strange skull might represent an early dog, a naturally distinctive wolf, or even an

22
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:50,000
unsuccessful attempt at domestication whose descendants disappeared. The safest evidence appears much later. By

23
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:58,000
at least 14,000 years ago, animals recognizable as dogs were living with humans in Europe. At Bon Obercastle in

24
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Germany, the remains of a young dog were buried with a man and a woman. This was not discarded food. It was placed in a

25
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:13,000
grave. Analysis of the dog's teeth suggests it suffered a severe illness, probably canine distemper, while still a

26
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:22,000
puppy. The disease can cause vomiting, diarrhea, fever, neurological problems, and death. Surviving for weeks would

27
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,000
likely have required humans to provide water, food, warmth, and cleaning. The puppy was too ill to hunt. It was

28
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
consuming resources while offering little immediate practical value. Yet, someone kept it alive. And after it

29
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:43,000
died, humans included it in a burial. By 14,000 years ago, the relationship had already moved beyond two species

30
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,000
temporarily cooperating. People cared about dogs. Genetics pushes the beginning further back, although it does

31
00:03:51,000 --> 00:04:00,000
not provide one universally accepted date. Dogs separated from the wolf lineage somewhere during the late pleaene. perhaps between about 20,000

32
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:10,000
and 40,000 years ago. By 11,000 years ago, several distinct dog lineages already existed across Eurasia. That

33
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:21,000
diversity means domestication must have started earlier. But where? Europe, Siberia, East Asia, Central Asia, and the Near East have all been proposed.

34
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Ancient DNA has eliminated some simple stories without producing a final address. Modern dogs descend from an ancient wolf population that no longer

35
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:36,000
survives in its original form. Living gray wolves are close relatives, not unchanged copies of the wolves that

36
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
became dogs. There may have been one primary domestication event followed by extensive mixing with other wolves.

37
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:49,000
[snorts] There may have been multiple early relationships with only one contributing most ancestry to later dogs.

38
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Populations moved, mixed, vanished, and replaced one another. The family tree was apparently drawn by an enthusiastic

39
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:06,000
wolf walking across wet ink. Still, researchers can reconstruct several plausible ways the relationship began.

40
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:17,000
The most familiar explanation is the camp scavenger hypothesis. Hunter gatherers killed large animals and left behind bones, skin, organs, and scraps.

41
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Wolves can smell food from great distances. Some would have approached human camps after people left or while everyone slept. Most wolves probably

42
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:33,000
remained cautious. The boldest came closer. That boldness carried both danger and reward. A wolf that moved too

43
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:42,000
close and behaved aggressively risked becoming tomorrow's fur blanket. But an animal calm enough to remain nearby without threatening anyone gained access

44
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:49,000
to concentrated food. Humans created a new ecological niche, the edge of camp.

45
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Generation after generation, wolves best able to exploit that niche would reproduce more successfully near people.

46
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:03,000
Natural selection began favoring lower fear, reduced aggression, and an ability to read human behavior. The wolves were

47
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:12,000
not choosing domestication. They were choosing leftovers. Humans, meanwhile, may have tolerated the least dangerous visitors. By the way, if you want to go

48
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:21,000
even deeper into the Secret Lives of Ancient Humans, I just launched a full course [music] called The Secret Lives of Ancient Humans. Ancient Minds Academy

49
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:31,000
gives you 10 exclusive videos, over 3 hours, and 30 minutes of content diving deep into the daily life, beliefs, [music] and struggles of early humans.

50
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Every video comes with a full PDF guide so you can explore at your own pace.

51
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,000
[music] Link is in the description, or you can scan this QR code directly.

52
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Don't forget to use code ancient 15 for 15% off. Wolves could clean waste that attracted other scavengers. Their

53
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:54,000
hearing and smell might warn of approaching predators or strangers. Even before becoming trained partners, they could function as a living alarm system.

54
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:02,000
This process is often called self-domemestication, but the term can be misleading. Wolves did not complete the transformation

55
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:11,000
alone. Humans still determined which animals were tolerated, fed, chased away, killed, adopted, or allowed to

56
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:20,000
breed. The relationship may have begun through wolf choices. It continued through human choices. There is also a

57
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,000
problem with the simple garbage dump story.

58
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:32,000
During the coldest stages of the ice age, mobile hunter gatherers may not have produced the large permanent waste piles found in later villages. Humans

59
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:43,000
and wolves also ate similar large prey, making them direct competitors. Why feed a rival when winter already treated every meal like a limited edition?

60
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Another hypothesis focuses on hunting.

61
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:54,000
Wolves were exceptional cooperative hunters. They could track animals by scent, pursue them through difficult terrain, and detect wounded prey. Humans

62
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:03,000
possessed projectile weapons, long-d distance planning, and the ability to kill efficiently once prey was located or cornered. Together, they may have

63
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:12,000
been more successful than either species alone. The first cooperation need not have looked like a trained modern hunting dog waiting for commands. Wolves

64
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:21,000
already understood group hunting. Humans may initially have followed wolves toward prey or allowed familiar animals to trail their hunts. Over time, each

65
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:29,000
side learned to predict the other. A cannid that remained close without stealing too aggressively could receive scraps. Humans who understood its

66
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:38,000
signals gained better information about prey and danger. The partnership grew from repeated moments when cooperation worked better than hostility. A third

67
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:48,000
possibility begins with puppies. Hunter gatherers sometimes captured the young of wild animals. A wolf pup taken early enough can become socialized to humans.

68
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
Children may have played with it. Adults may have valued it for ritual.

69
00:08:52,000 --> 00:09:01,000
curiosity, companionship, or future hunting ability. Most adopted wolves would have become difficult as they matured. Wolves are not simply large

70
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:10,000
dogs waiting for improved public relations. They are powerful, wary animals with strong prey drives and behaviors shaped for life in a wolfpack.

71
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:20,000
But some individuals would have been unusually manageable. If humans kept and bred those animals, perhaps pairing the calmst with one another, selection could move quickly.

72
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Even without understanding heredity, people would notice that offspring from tolerant parents were easier to raise.

73
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:34,000
The true beginning may have combined all three processes. Some wolves approached camps. Humans occasionally adopted pups.

74
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:45,000
Familiar animals joined hunts. Useful or friendly individuals received food and protection. Aggressive ones were excluded. Wolves and humans gradually entered one another's social worlds.

75
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:55,000
There was no contract. There were thousands of small decisions. Do not throw a spear at that one. Leave it the bone. Let the pup stay near the fire.

76
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:59,000
Follow it when it becomes alert. Keep its offspring.

77
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:06,000
Those choices accumulated until a group of wolves could no longer thrive in exactly the same way as wild wolves.

78
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:13,000
Behavior probably changed before appearance. Animals living near humans needed a higher tolerance for unfamiliar

79
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:21,000
people, noise, handling, and crowded conditions. They had to respond to human gestures and emotional signals. Reduced

80
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:31,000
fear allowed them to remain close enough to learn. Dogs became remarkably skilled at reading us. Many can follow a human pointing gesture toward hidden food.

81
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:39,000
They monitor faces, voices, posture, and attention. They also direct communication toward humans. looking

82
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:45,000
back when confronted with an unsolvable problem. Some wolves raised intensively by people can perform impressive social

83
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:53,000
tasks, so domestication did not create cooperation from nothing. Wolves already depended on complex teamwork within

84
00:10:53,000 --> 00:11:02,000
packs. Dogs redirected those social abilities toward another species. This was the crucial change. The earliest

85
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:13,000
dogs probably still looked wolflike, but once populations were isolated and humans controlled reproduction, physical differences accumulated. Dog skulls generally became shorter and broader.

86
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
Teeth became smaller and more crowded.

87
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Bodies varied in size. Reproductive cycles changed. Barking became more common in adulthood. Features associated with juvenile behavior persisted longer.

88
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
This process is called neotini.

89
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:35,000
Adult animals retain traits that were more typical of younger ancestors.

90
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:42,000
Playfulness, attention seeking, and dependence on human caregivers can remain throughout a dog's life. Their

91
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:49,000
appearance diversified, too. Floppy ears, curled tails, spotted coats, and

92
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:54,000
shorter faces became common in many domesticated populations.

93
00:11:54,000 --> 00:12:02,000
These traits are often grouped under domestication syndrome. Although scientists continue debating whether they arise through one shared biological

94
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:10,000
mechanism or through several different forms of selection, humans were initially selecting behavior. But

95
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:20,000
behavior, hormones, development, and anatomy are connected. Choosing animals that reacted less fearfully could indirectly influence other traits.

96
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:29,000
Later, people deliberately selected coat color, size, hunting style, strength, or appearance.

97
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:36,000
The wolf was slowly redesigned without anyone possessing a blueprint. Dogs also changed human life. They helped detect

98
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:44,000
predators and rival groups. They tracked prey, the guarded camps, transported loads in some regions, provided warmth,

99
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:54,000
consumed refues, and sometimes became emergency food. That final role is less heartwarming, but extremely prehistoric.

100
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:02,000
More importantly, dogs extended human senses. A human could see well in daylight and plan far into the future. A

101
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:09,000
dog could hear movement in darkness and smell an animal that had crossed the ground hours earlier. Together, they formed a combined hunting and

102
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:14,000
surveillance system. This may have helped humans enter new environments.

103
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Dogs spread alongside people across Eurasia and eventually into the Americas. Their histories often follow human migrations, although dog

104
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:30,000
populations were sometimes replaced even when the humans remained. They were not cargo. They were fellow travelers, but

105
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:38,000
the first domestication differed from everything that followed. Dogs appeared before agriculture. Humans domesticated

106
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:45,000
them while still hunting wild animals and gathering plants. Sheep, goats, pigs, and cattle emerged later as

107
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:54,000
communities began settling more permanently and managing food production. This required another kind of relationship. Wolves probably entered

108
00:13:54,000 --> 00:14:00,000
through what researchers call the commensal pathway. They moved into human- created environments and

109
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:06,000
gradually formed closer bonds. Livestock generally followed the prey pathway.

110
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:13,000
Humans had hunted wild sheep, goats, cattle, and pigs for millennia. They knew where these animals lived, how they

111
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:19,000
moved, when they reproduced, and which individuals were easiest to kill.

112
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:26,000
Domestication began when hunting became management. Imagine a community repeatedly hunting the same goat

113
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:33,000
population. Killing animals randomly could destroy the herd. But hunters who spared breeding females and young

114
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:42,000
animals while targeting certain adult males preserved future meat. That pattern leaves evidence in bones. In a

115
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:50,000
natural herd, males and females survive in proportions shaped by ecology. In a managed herd, humans often kill many

116
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:57,000
young males because only a few are needed for breeding. Females are kept longer so they can produce offspring.

117
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:05,000
Atsao archaeological sites in southwest Asia changing age and sex profiles reveal the selective killing before the

118
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:14,000
animals bodies became obviously domestic. The earliest herders were altering populations behaviorally before evolution altered their skeletons. Goat

119
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:23,000
management was underway around 11,000 years ago in the fertile crescent, especially around the Zagros mountains and parts of Anatolia. The wild ancestor

120
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:31,000
was the bezor goat, a powerful mountain animal with long curved horns. Early people did not necessarily capture an

121
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:40,000
entire herd and place it behind a perfect fence. They may have controlled movement through landscape features, guarded animals, removed selected

122
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:44,000
individuals, and protected herds from predators.

123
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:53,000
Over generations, animals living under human control became separated from wild populations. Humans favored goats that tolerated crowding and proximity.

124
00:15:53,000 --> 00:16:01,000
Animals that panicked constantly, attacked handlers, escaped, or refused to reproduce in captivity were less

125
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:09,000
likely to leave descendants in managed herds. Nobody needed to say, "Let us genetically reduce goat aggression. They

126
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:17,000
merely kept the goats that made Tuesday less exhausting." Sheep underwent a similar transition in the northern fertile crescent. Their ancestors were

127
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:25,000
wild mulon-like sheep, not the fluffy creatures seen on modern farms. Early domestic sheep were raised mainly for

128
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:32,000
meat. Their coats were not initially enormous masses of continuously growing wool. Wild sheep naturally shed much of

129
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:41,000
their coat. The thick woolly fleece familiar today emerged through later selective breeding, probably thousands of years after management began. This

130
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:49,000
pattern appears repeatedly. The first reason humans domesticated an animal was not always the reason later civilizations valued it most. Cattle

131
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:57,000
came from a enormous wild boines that stood taller and carried far more dangerous horns than most modern cattle.

132
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:04,000
Adult males could weigh close to a metric ton. Domestication did not begin by locating the gentlest cow. It began

133
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:13,000
with an animal capable of turning the farmer into an archaeological layer. A small number of oroxs appear to have contributed to the early tarin cattle

134
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:24,000
domesticated in the near east around 10,500 years ago. Other cattle traditions emerged elsewhere, including zebu domestication in South Asia.

135
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:29,000
Managing oroxs required control over breeding, movement, and aggression.

136
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:36,000
Humans selected smaller, more tractable individuals, whether deliberately or simply because those were the ones they

137
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:42,000
could keep. Cattle eventually supplied meat, hides, milk, manure, and labor.

138
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:52,000
But those uses developed at different times. Milking requires knowledge about reproduction, lactation, storage, and the human digestion of dairy.

139
00:17:52,000 --> 00:18:01,000
Using cattle to pull plows or carts requires harnesses, training, and sufficiently strong settlements to exploit animal power. Domestication

140
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:09,000
created possibilities. It did not unlock every feature immediately. Pigs followed a messier route. Wild boars were hunted

141
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:17,000
prey, but they were also opportunistic scavengers attracted to human settlements. They could eat discarded food and forage around fields. This

142
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:25,000
means pig domestication may have combined the prey and commensal pathways. Humans managed some wild boores, transported them, and controlled

143
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:31,000
breeding. At the same time, wild populations continued mixing with domestic ones.

144
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:40,000
Ancient DNA shows that the pigs carried into Europe by early farmers later interbred extensively with European wild boores. Much of their original

145
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:49,000
Neareastern ancestry was eventually replaced even though the animals remained domestic. A domestic population can absorb wild genes without becoming

146
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:54,000
wild again. Domestication is a relationship, not a purity certificate.

147
00:18:54,000 --> 00:19:01,000
Cats demonstrate this more clearly. As humans formed permanent settlements, stored food attracted house mice. Mice

148
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:10,000
arrived because villages offered shelter and calories. Wild cats arrived because villages had conveniently gathered their prey into one location. Humans did not

149
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:18,000
need to train cats to hunt mice. Cats had completed that course before attending. The least fearful African wild cats began tolerating human

150
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:26,000
settlements. People benefited because the cats killed rodents. Cats benefited because human grain stores supported

151
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:33,000
dense prey populations. This was another commensal pathway. Evidence from Cyprus shows how early the relationship

152
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:42,000
developed. Wild cats were not native to the island, so humans had to transport them there by boat. By around 9,500

153
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:52,000
years ago, a cat was buried near a human at Schiller Cambos. Cats may have been carried to Cypress even earlier, probably for pest control.

154
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:59,000
Unlike dogs, however, cats remained physically and behaviorally close to their wild ancestors.

155
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:07,000
Humans exerted less control over their feeding and breeding. Useful cats could live near settlements while still choosing mates independently.

156
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:15,000
Strong selection for dramatic physical change came much later, particularly when people began breeding cats for appearance.

157
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:23,000
This is why a house cat can spend the morning sleeping on a cushion and the afternoon behaving like a small fully operational predator. It was

158
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:30,000
domesticated under unusually flexible terms. These different histories reveal that humans did not possess one method

159
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:38,000
for domesticating animals. Researchers often describe three broad pathways. The commonal pathway began when animals such

160
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:46,000
as wolves and wild cats entered human environments for food. The relationship became closer as tolerant individuals

161
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:53,000
benefited. The prey pathway began when hunters changed how they exploited animals such as sheep, goats, cattle,

162
00:20:53,000 --> 00:21:04,000
and pigs. Selective hunting became herd management and management became controlled breeding. The directed pathway appeared later after humans already understood animal husbandry.

163
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:12,000
People deliberately domesticated new species for particular purposes. Horses are a good example of a later, more directed relationship. Although their

164
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:19,000
early history remains complex, they provided meat and milk before becoming essential for riding, transport, and

165
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:27,000
warfare. Donkeys were recruited for carrying loads. Camels made long-d distanceance movement through dry regions more practical. By then, humans

166
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:37,000
knew that animals could be managed across generations. The first wolf partnership had required entering the unknown. Not every species could follow.

167
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:44,000
Humans have hunted thousands of animals but domesticated relatively few. A useful species must possess a workable

168
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:52,000
combination of traits. It needs a diet humans can supply a reproductive cycle manageable in captivity, sufficient

169
00:21:52,000 --> 00:22:00,000
growth and behavior that does not make controlled breeding impossible. Social structure matters. Herd animals often

170
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:05,000
already follow leaders, tolerate other members, and live in groups.

171
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:14,000
Humans can insert themselves into that system. Territorial solitary animals are harder to manage at scale. Fear matters, too.

172
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:19,000
Some species panic so violently in confinement that they injure themselves.

173
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:26,000
Others become dangerously aggressive during breeding. Some refuse to reproduce. Some mature too slowly.

174
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:35,000
Others are expensive to feed because they require meat. People can tame elephants, cheetahs, bears, and many other animals.

175
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:42,000
But repeated taming is not the same as establishing a self-sustaining domestic population. Zebras make the difficulty

176
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:49,000
obvious. They resemble horses and eat similar food, but they evolved under heavy predation pressure in Africa. They

177
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:56,000
can be highly reactive, bite aggressively, and deliver powerful kicks. a zebra can be trained.

178
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:07,000
Turning thousands of zebras into a reliably manageable breeding population is a different project, especially when horses already exist and are less committed to workplace violence.

179
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:16,000
Domestication also created costs. Dense herds overgrazed landscapes and competed with humans for crops or water. Guarding

180
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:24,000
animals required labor. Stored fodder attracted pests. Livestock could be stolen, wander away, or die during

181
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:33,000
drought. Most importantly, living closely with animals increased opportunities for pathogens to cross species boundaries. Not every human

182
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:41,000
disease came from livestock, and domestication did not instantly generate epidemics. But larger settlements, dense

183
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:52,000
animal populations, waste accumulation, and frequent contact created new pathways for infection. Humans received meat, milk, hides, labor, and transport.

184
00:23:52,000 --> 00:24:01,000
Microbes reviewed the arrangement and also found opportunities. The animals paid a greater price. Domestication placed their reproduction increasingly

185
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:09,000
under human control. Males, judged unnecessary, were killed young or castrated. Herds were confined, moved,

186
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:17,000
traded, and selectively bred. bodies changed to produce more meat, milk, wool, eggs, or labor than natural

187
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:25,000
selection would normally favor. Some animals gained protection from predators and access to reliable food. They also became property. This makes

188
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:33,000
domestication neither a simple friendship nor a single act of domination. It was a long evolutionary

189
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:41,000
relationship in which both species changed, but one side gradually acquired far more control. Humans changed too.

190
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:46,000
Adult mammals usually lose much of their ability to digest lactose after infancy.

191
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:54,000
In populations with long histories of dairying, genetic variance allowing lactase production into adulthood became more common. Animal domestication

192
00:24:54,000 --> 00:25:04,000
changed human diet strongly enough to shape human evolution. Pasturalism allowed people to inhabit grasslands where farming was difficult. Cattle and

193
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:14,000
sheep converted vegetation humans could not digest into meat and milk. Horses, donkeys, and camels transformed travel.

194
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:23,000
Oxen multiplied the force available for plowing and hauling. Settlements accumulated more food, property, and inequality.

195
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:26,000
Livestock became wealth that could walk.

196
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:34,000
Animals influenced marriage payments, inheritance, warfare, religion, trade, and political power.

197
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:43,000
A process that may have begun with wolves stealing scraps eventually reorganized entire civilizations. Yet, archaeology rarely captures the

198
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:52,000
beginning cleanly. Bones become smaller gradually. Horn shapes change. Kill patterns shift. Genetic lineages mix.

199
00:25:52,000 --> 00:26:00,000
Wild and domestic animals interbreed. A settlement may manage a herd for centuries before the animals look clearly different. Domestication is

200
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:04,000
easiest to recognize after it has already happened.

201
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:08,000
The earliest stage hides inside ordinary behavior.

202
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:17,000
A hunter spares a female goat. A child feeds a wolf pup. A family tolerates cats near stored grain.

203
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:26,000
A community moves animals instead of immediately killing them. No single decision creates a domestic species. But each changes which animals survive

204
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:39,000
around humans. Repeated across generations, those choices become evolution. This is why asking who domesticated the first animal may never produce one person, village, or date.

205
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:45,000
The first dogs emerged from a lost population of wolves somewhere in ice age Eurasia.

206
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:52,000
The process began well before the first definite dog burial around 14,000 years ago and possibly thousands of years

207
00:26:52,000 --> 00:27:01,000
earlier. Humans probably did not drag adult wolves into camp and forced them to become loyal. The wolves most capable of approaching humans entered first.

208
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:14,000
People tolerated, fed, adopted, hunted beside, and eventually bred them. Fear declined. Communication improved. Bodies changed. A predator became a partner.

209
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:22,000
Then, after humans began settling and producing food, domestication followed new routes. Hunters managed goats and

210
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:30,000
sheep. Communities controlled cattle and pigs. Grain stores attracted mice and mice attracted cats. Every species

211
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:37,000
entered for a different reason. Dogs may have helped humans hunt before farms existed. Goats and sheep turned

212
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:45,000
difficult landscapes into food. Cattle brought enormous strength. Pigs converted waste into meat. Cats arrived

213
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:52,000
because the mice had opened a restaurant. What united them was not obedience. It was compatibility.

214
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:59,000
Certain animals could tolerate living near humans. Humans could influence their reproduction. Both species

215
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:08,000
received enough benefit for the relationship to continue. Once that cycle began, natural selection and human choice pulled the population away from

216
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:19,000
its wild ancestors. The most important step was not capturing an animal. Humans had captured animals forever. It was allowing the relationship to reproduce.

217
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:26,000
A tame wolf dies and the experiment ends. A population of tolerant wolves raises tolerant offspring near human

218
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:37,000
camps and a new evolutionary path begins. That path eventually produced animals no ice age hunter could have imagined. Tiny dogs carried in bags.

219
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:39,000
Sheep unable to shed their own wool.

220
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:47,000
Cattle producing enormous quantities of milk. Pigs heavier than wild boores. and cats with no hair despite showing

221
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:54,000
absolutely no interest in sweaters. But beneath all those extreme forms lies the same ancient process. Humans created a

222
00:28:54,000 --> 00:29:01,000
new environment. Some animals entered it. The individuals best able to survive beside us produced offspring. And humans

223
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:08,000
increasingly decided which offspring those would be. We did not domesticate the first animals in one brilliant

224
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:16,000
moment. We changed their world. And then we kept choosing who belonged in it. The first wolf that approached a camp

225
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:22,000
probably wanted nothing more profound than meat. The first human who tolerated it may have wanted cleaner waste, an

226
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:30,000
alarm in the darkness, or simply the company of an unusual pup. Neither could understand what had started. Thousands

227
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:37,000
of generations later, the wolf's descendants would guard homes, guide hunters, pull sleds, detect disease,

228
00:29:37,000 --> 00:29:46,000
herd livestock, sleep beside children, and wait under dinner tables with the patient expression of an animal certain that history owes it one more piece of

229
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:49,000
food. The first domestication was not the conquest of nature. It was a dangerous animal moving slightly closer to the fire, and a human deciding not to drive it away.
